<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice of Villafranca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Villafranca · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-italian-war-of-independence">Second Italian War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Armistice of Villafranca was agreed on 11 July 1859 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> after the battles of Magenta and Solferino. Austria gave up most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, which passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>. Made without consulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, who resigned in protest, its terms were confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zurich">Treaty of Zurich</a> in November 1859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-villafranca.jpg" alt="Armistice of Villafranca" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armistice_of_Villafranca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had allied with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> on 26 January 1859, and the treaty promised Sardinia all of Lombardy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> if the war allowed. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> declared war on 24 April, the allies won at Magenta on 4 June and at Solferino on 24 June, and the Austrian army fell back beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mincio">Mincio</a>. Napoleon III then faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> mobilising six army corps on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, an indifferent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> unwilling to go further, and opposition at home from his foreign minister Alexandre Walewski, Empress Eugénie and conservative circles. On 6 July he sent General Émile Félix Fleury to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> with a proposal for a truce.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Commissioners agreed a truce at Villafranca on 8 July, to last until 16 August. On 11 July the two emperors met alone at the Gandini Morelli Bugna palace, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> presented as British conditions terms he had suggested himself. Prince Napoléon carried the draft to Verona, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> signed only after it was settled that the fortresses of the Quadrilatero and the Province of Mantua stayed Austrian and that the old rulers might be restored by force.</p><p>The two sovereigns would favour an Italian confederation under the pope, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> would join it while remaining Austrian, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> and the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> would return with an amnesty, and the pope would be asked for reforms in the Papal States. Victor Emmanuel II signed on 12 July with the clause for all that concerns me. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, told of the proposals on the afternoon of 11 July, resigned, and that night spoke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s betrayal in a stormy scene with the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Only the transfer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> was carried out. Pro-Piedmontese provisional governments installed in Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Parma and the Papal Legations refused to restore the old rulers, and plebiscites in Modena and Parma voted for annexation; the confederation and the papal reforms never came about. The loss of Lombardy, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> since 1706, was its gravest defeat on the Italian question.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> remained both protector of the Papal States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>'s main ally, which kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>'s influence over Italian affairs. Recalled to office on 21 January 1860, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> accepted the French price for annexing central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>: by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> of 24 March 1860 Sardinia ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and Savoy to France and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, what is now Emilia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>, and Tuscany.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Armistice of Villafranca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of 1859 brought Lombardy by the <b>Armistice of Villafranca</b> and the Treaty of Zurich; in 1860 plebiscites joined Tuscany, Parma, Modena and the Romagna, while Savoy and Nice were ceded to France by the Treaty of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Austrians lost at Magenta and Solferino, and by the <b>Armistice of Villafranca</b> on 12 July Lombardy went to Sardinia while Austria kept the Veneto and Mantua.</blockquote><a href="/movement/unification-of-italy">Unification of Italy · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Their assemblies voted to join the Kingdom of Sardinia, defying the <b>Peace of Villafranca</b>, which had foreseen the return of the old rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-provinces-of-central-italy">United Provinces of Central Italy</a></li></ul></section>
